<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened runtime entitlements for the Phosphor desktop shell.

  WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. The keystore encrypts the keys at rest. That moves them off disk and
  into the heap of a running process, and on macOS a same-user process can attach to another
  with task_for_pid and read that heap. Encryption at rest without a hardened runtime therefore
  moves the key from a file anyone can read to a heap anyone can read, which is not a control.
  The two ship together or neither is real.

  THE ENTITLEMENT THAT IS NOT HERE IS THE POINT. `com.apple.security.cs.get-task-allow` is
  absent, and absent is what matters: it is the entitlement that lets a debugger attach to this
  process. Do not add it, not even temporarily, and not for a "debug build" that might get
  shipped. `com.apple.security.cs.debugger` is denied explicitly for the same reason, written
  out rather than omitted so that granting it is a visible edit.

  ALLOW-JIT IS REQUIRED AND IS NOT A WEAKENING OF THE ABOVE. Settled at the first signed build,
  2026-09-09. It was `<false/>`, and with it false the bundle could not run at all: the shell
  window opened, the node child was spawned, and it died immediately with

      Fatal process out of memory: Failed to reserve virtual memory for CodeRange

  which is V8 being refused the executable memory it needs to exist. Every signed build before
  this one had a working window and a dead backend. It is a different capability from debugger
  attach: allow-jit permits this process to map its OWN memory executable through MAP_JIT, and
  grants nothing to any other process. The control that stops another process reading these
  keys is the absence of get-task-allow, and that is untouched.

  `com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory` stays denied. V8 uses MAP_JIT
  properly, so it does not need the blanket version, and the health check below passes without
  it.

  LIBRARY VALIDATION: SETTLED, 2026-09-09, and the answer was that the app does not need it
  disabled. `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation` used to be `<true/>` here, on the
  usual grounds that the bundle ships its own node in `externalBin`. It does not need to be.
  `tauri build` signs the sidecar with the same identity as the app, so a same-team sidecar
  satisfies library validation on its own, and the payload's dependencies (viem, zod and the MCP
  SDK) are pure JavaScript that load no third-party dylibs.

  The check that settled it is the one the previous note specified, and it is not "the app
  launches": library validation bites the CHILD, and a shell whose backend never spawned still
  shows you a window. The check is that the backend is alive, which the app answers with no
  token and no secret:

      curl 127.0.0.1:4177/api/health

  Run on the first signed build with the key removed, that returned
  `{"ok":true,"version":"0.3.0","killSwitch":false,"pending":0,"locked":true,"auditChain":"ok"}`
  with the node child running under `Phosphor.app/Contents/MacOS/node`. So the key is gone and
  library validation is enforced.

  One caveat that outlives the measurement: it describes today's dependency tree. A native
  dependency added later would change the answer silently, and the symptom would be a window
  with a dead backend rather than a crash. If that ever happens, run the health check above
  before reaching for this file.

  A NOTE ON EDITING THIS COMMENT. codesign parses it with AMFI's XML reader, which is stricter
  than plutil: a double hyphen inside an XML comment is illegal XML, and while `plutil -lint`
  accepts it, codesign fails the whole build with "Failed to parse entitlements:
  AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error". A command-line flag written out longhand here cost one
  build exactly that way. Do not put a double hyphen in this comment.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <false/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.debugger</key>
  <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
